Import method
-------------

  A blob-filtered, single-commit (depth 1) sparse checkout of the paths
  below, copied without modification into this component:

    include/                -> libc/include/
    lib/libc/               -> libc/lib/libc/
    lib/libpthread/         -> libc/lib/libpthread/
    sys/sys/                -> libc/sys/sys/                 (dependency)
    sys/arch/amd64/include/ -> libc/sys/arch/amd64/include/  (dependency)
    sys/arch/i386/include/  -> libc/sys/arch/i386/include/
    sys/arch/arm/include/   -> libc/sys/arch/arm/include/
    sys/arch/arm64/include/ -> libc/sys/arch/arm64/include/
    sys/arch/armv7/include/ -> libc/sys/arch/armv7/include/
    sys/arch/riscv64/include/ -> libc/sys/arch/riscv64/include/
    sys/arch/powerpc64/include/ -> libc/sys/arch/powerpc64/include/
    sys/arch/sparc64/include/ -> libc/sys/arch/sparc64/include/
    regress/lib/libc/       -> libc/regress/lib/libc/        (test harness)

  sys/sys and the sys/arch/*/include trees are not libc, but the public
  libc headers include <sys/*.h> and <machine/*.h>, so the corresponding
  kernel headers are needed for the tree to be self-hosting for
  compilation and for the host-side tests.  The architectures imported
  are the ones whose machine headers are needed to check the new headers
  across OpenBSD's supported platforms (see README).  These sys/ subtrees
  are byte-for-byte upstream and are not part of the change set.

  src/lib/libpthread in OpenBSD -current contains only manual pages: the
  thread implementation lives in lib/libc/thread/ (see
  lib/libc/thread/Makefile.inc).  The imported libpthread/ directory is
  therefore only the man pages, and the C11 <threads.h> implementation in
  this tree is built into libc, next to the pthread implementation it
  wraps.

